IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mtpd-dates`
IMPORT `mtpd-rules`

-- Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) -- the questions of the PERSON WHOSE TREATMENT IT IS.
--
-- SEAT: the individual the Act is applied to -- the person who gives an advance care directive, appoints a medical
-- treatment decision maker or a support person, and who may one day be treated, or be the subject of research, while
-- unable to decide. "Do I have decision-making capacity?", "Is my advance care directive valid, and in force?", "Who
-- decides for me if I cannot?", "Can I be treated without consent?", "Is the practitioner protected from my claim?", "By
-- when must I apply for a rehearing?". Health practitioners, VCAT, the Public Advocate and ethics committees get no seat:
-- their beliefs, opinions, consents and orders are asked as facts. Where a family member or decision maker asks on the
-- person's behalf, the questions are still worded from the person's seat.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of mtpd-rules.l4.
-- The two rules with deadlines (ss 67(2), 88(3)) are DEONTIC rules run inside; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Medical
-- Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016, Version No. 012 (1 July 2024), not the authorised version. (c) State of
-- Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced
-- except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026
-- (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016: the questions of the person whose treatment it is`

§§ `Decision-making capacity`

@export Do I have decision-making capacity for a medical treatment decision (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 4)? An adult is presumed to have it unless there is evidence to the contrary; otherwise a person has it if they can understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate the decision, with any practicable and appropriate support. Capacity is decision by decision, and may be temporary.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is any evidence that you may not have capacity for this particular decision (not just your appearance or a decision others think unwise)
      `understand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can understand the information relevant to the decision and its effect, if it is explained in a way suited to you
      `retain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can retain that information long enough to make the decision
      `weigh` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can use or weigh that information in making the decision
      `communicate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can communicate the decision and your views in some way (speech, gestures or other means), with any support you need
GIVETH A `Whether I have decision-making capacity`
`Do I have decision-making capacity` MEANS
    `s 4 -- my decision-making capacity` `age` `evidence` `understand` `retain` `weigh` `communicate`

@export Can this person apply to VCAT about my capacity, my advance care directive, my decision maker or support person, or a treatment decision about me (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 3, "eligible applicant"; ss 5, 22, 43, 65)? Only a health practitioner caring for me, my decision maker, my support person, the Public Advocate, or someone VCAT is satisfied has a special interest. I am not on the list myself, though I am a party to the proceeding.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who is applying` @desc Who wants to apply: you yourself; a health practitioner caring for or treating you; your medical treatment decision maker; your support person; the Public Advocate; someone VCAT is satisfied has a special interest in your affairs; or anyone else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this person apply to VCAT about me` MEANS
    `s 3 -- an eligible applicant` `who`

§§ `My advance care directive`

@export Is my advance care directive validly made (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 13, 14, 16, 17, 21, 22)? It needs capacity and understanding for each statement, writing in English with your full name, date of birth and address, your signature (or an adult non-witness signing at your direction), two adult witnesses signing in your and each other's presence, one of them a registered medical practitioner, neither your appointed decision maker, and their certificates. A document that fails does not take effect as a directive unless VCAT declares it valid, but can still show your preferences and values.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years when you gave the directive
      `capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had decision-making capacity for, and understood the nature and effect of, each statement in it when you gave it
      `written` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is in writing in English and includes your full name, date of birth and address
      `signer` IS A `Who signed the document` @desc Who signed it: you yourself; an adult who is not a witness, at your direction; someone else at your direction (a child or a witness); or nobody
      `witnesses` IS A NUMBER @desc How many adult witnesses witnessed the signing
      `together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each witness signed and dated it in your presence and in each other's presence
      `doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at least one witness is a registered medical practitioner who wrote that qualification on the document
      `decision maker witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether either witness is your appointed medical treatment decision maker
      `trained witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were under 18: whether a witness is a registered medical practitioner or psychologist with the prescribed training and experience (answer yes if you were 18 or over)
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each witness certified on the document your apparent capacity and understanding, that you signed freely and voluntarily in front of both of them, and that they are not your appointed decision maker
      `induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone induced you to give it by dishonesty or undue influence
      `order` IS A `VCAT order on validity` @desc Whether VCAT has made an order about its validity: none; declared valid despite a failure to comply; or declared invalid
GIVETH A `Whether my advance care directive is valid`
`Is my advance care directive valid` MEANS
    `ss 13, 16, 17, 21, 22 -- whether my advance care directive is valid` `age` `capacity` `written` `signer` `witnesses` `together` `doctor` `decision maker witness` `trained witness` `certified` `induced` `order`

@export Is my advance care directive in force on a given day (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 19, 20, 22)? It comes into force when signed and stays in force until any expiry date it states or until it is revoked (a later directive revokes an earlier one); VCAT can suspend it. Assumes the directive was validly made.
GIVEN `signed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the directive was signed and witnessed
      `has expiry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the directive states an expiry date
      `expiry` IS A DATE @desc The expiry date it states (any date if none)
      `revocation` IS A `How my directive was revoked` @desc Whether it has been revoked: not revoked; by a later advance care directive you gave; by a revocation made with the formalities of Part 2; or in whole by VCAT
      `revoked on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was revoked (any date if not revoked)
      `suspended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has suspended it for a period that includes the day asked about
      `day` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about
GIVETH A `Whether my advance care directive is in force`
`Is my advance care directive in force` MEANS
    `ss 19, 20, 22 -- whether my advance care directive is in force` `signed on` `has expiry` `expiry` `revocation` `revoked on` `suspended` `day`

@export Is this statement in my advance care directive a binding instructional directive, a values directive, or void (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 6, 8A, 12, 18)? Only a statement expressly identified as an instructional directive, clear in its application, about ordinary treatment, binds as my own consent or refusal; consent to a special medical procedure, palliative care, and statements in interstate directives are values directives; unlawful or prescribed statements are void and severed.
GIVEN `subject` IS A `What the statement is about` @desc What the statement is about: medical treatment or a medical research procedure; consent to a special medical procedure; palliative care; or voluntary assisted dying
      `expressly instructional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document expressly identifies the statement, on its face, as an instructional directive
      `clear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement is clear and certain in how it applies to the circumstances
      `interstate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement is in a directive made in another State or Territory and recognised in Victoria under section 95
      `unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement is unlawful or would require an unlawful act
      `breach of standards` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving effect to it would make a health practitioner breach a professional standard or code of conduct
      `prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a statement of a kind, or contains an instruction, that regulations under the Act prohibit
GIVETH A `What my statement is`
`What is this statement in my advance care directive` MEANS
    `ss 6, 8A, 12, 18 -- what my statement is` `subject` `expressly instructional` `clear` `interstate` `unlawful` `breach of standards` `prescribed`

@export Is my advance care directive or appointment made in another State or Territory recognised in Victoria (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 95-97)? It is, if it complies with the law where it was made, to the extent it could have been made under the Victorian Act; a recognised interstate directive's statements count as values directives, not binding instructions.
GIVEN `instrument` IS A `Interstate instrument` @desc Which instrument: an advance care directive made in another State or Territory; or an appointment of a decision maker or support person made there
      `complies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it complies with the requirements of the State or Territory where it was made
      `extent` IS A `How far my instrument could have been made in Victoria` @desc How much of it could validly have been made under the Victorian Act: everything; only part; or none
GIVETH A `Whether my interstate instrument is recognised`
`Is my interstate instrument recognised in Victoria` MEANS
    `ss 95-97 -- whether my interstate instrument is recognised` `instrument` `complies` `extent`

§§ `My medical treatment decision maker and support person`

@export Is my appointment of a medical treatment decision maker or support person valid (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 26-45)? A decision maker must be an adult appointed by an adult with capacity; a support person may be anyone, appointed by anyone with capacity. The appointment must be in writing in English, signed, witnessed by two adults (one an authorised witness, such as a doctor or an authorised affidavit taker), certified, and accepted in writing by each appointee before it takes effect. VCAT can declare it effective or invalid.
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of appointment` @desc Which appointment: an appointed medical treatment decision maker; or a support person
      `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years when you made the appointment
      `capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had decision-making capacity when you made it
      `appointee age` IS A NUMBER @desc The age in whole years of the person you appointed (for several, the youngest)
      `written` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is in writing in English with your full name, date of birth and address and any details of each appointee the regulations require
      `signer` IS A `Who signed the document` @desc Who signed it: you yourself; an adult who is not a witness or an appointee, at your direction; someone else at your direction (a child, a witness or an appointee); or nobody
      `witnesses` IS A NUMBER @desc How many adult witnesses witnessed the signing
      `together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each witness signed and dated it in your presence and in each other's presence
      `authorised witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at least one witness is a registered medical practitioner or an authorised affidavit taker who wrote that qualification on the document
      `appointee witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a witness is one of the people you appointed
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each witness certified on it your apparent capacity and understanding, that you signed freely and voluntarily in front of both of them, and that they are not an appointee
      `accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each appointee has signed a written acceptance on the same document, witnessed by an adult, in the words the Act requires
      `induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone induced you to make the appointment by dishonesty or undue influence
      `order` IS A `VCAT order on an appointment` @desc Whether VCAT has made an order: none; declared the attempt to appoint effective; or declared the appointment invalid
GIVETH A `Whether my appointment is valid`
`Is my appointment valid` MEANS
    `ss 26-45 -- whether my appointment is valid` `kind` `age` `capacity` `appointee age` `written` `signer` `witnesses` `together` `authorised witness` `appointee witness` `certified` `accepted` `induced` `order`

@export Is my appointment of a medical treatment decision maker or support person still in force (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 30, 35, 38, 39, 43)? It lasts until I revoke it (with capacity, and with the same formalities as making it), the appointee resigns in a signed, dated and witnessed writing, or VCAT revokes it; appointing a new support person revokes the old one.
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of appointment` @desc Which appointment: an appointed medical treatment decision maker; or a support person
      `revocation` IS A `My revocation of the appointment` @desc Whether you have revoked it: not revoked; revoked with capacity and the formalities; tried without capacity for that decision; or tried without the formalities
      `later support person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have since appointed another support person
      `resignation` IS A `The appointee's resignation` @desc Whether the appointee has resigned: no; in a signed and dated writing witnessed by one adult; or without meeting those requirements
      `VCAT revoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT has made an order revoking the appointment
GIVETH A `Whether my appointment is in force`
`Is my appointment in force` MEANS
    `ss 30, 35, 38, 39 -- whether my appointment is in force` `kind` `revocation` `later support person` `resignation` `VCAT revoked`

@export Who is my medical treatment decision maker if I cannot decide (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 55)? In order: my appointed decision maker; a VCAT-appointed guardian with medical treatment power; then the first of my spouse or domestic partner, my primary carer, my oldest adult child, my oldest parent, my oldest adult sibling who is in a close and continuing relationship with me and is reasonably available, willing and able. For a child, a parent or guardian. Not while I am a mental health patient.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a patient under the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022 at the time
      `appointee available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have an appointed medical treatment decision maker (including an old medical enduring power or enduring guardian treated as one) who is reasonably available and willing and able to decide
      `guardian available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a guardian appointed by VCAT with power to make medical treatment decisions for you is reasonably available and willing and able
      `partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your spouse or domestic partner is in a close and continuing relationship with you and is reasonably available and willing and able
      `carer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your primary carer (an adult in a care relationship with principal responsibility for your care) is in a close and continuing relationship with you and is reasonably available and willing and able
      `adult child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an adult child of yours is in a close and continuing relationship with you and is reasonably available and willing and able
      `parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent of yours (or, if you are under 18, a parent, guardian or other person with parental responsibility) is in a close and continuing relationship with you and is reasonably available and willing and able
      `adult sibling` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an adult brother or sister of yours is in a close and continuing relationship with you and is reasonably available and willing and able
GIVETH A `Who my medical treatment decision maker is`
`Who is my medical treatment decision maker` MEANS
    `s 55 -- who my medical treatment decision maker is` `age` `mental health patient` `appointee available` `guardian available` `partner` `carer` `adult child` `parent` `adult sibling`

@export May my medical treatment decision maker or support person get my health information (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 94)? Yes, for information relevant to a medical treatment decision that I could lawfully collect myself, and a health practitioner may disclose it to them.
GIVEN `role` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person asking is your medical treatment decision maker or your support person
      `relevant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is relevant to a medical treatment decision to be made by you, your decision maker or your support person
      `lawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you could lawfully collect the information yourself
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May my decision maker or support person get my health information` MEANS
    `s 94 -- my decision maker or support person may get my health information` `role` `relevant` `lawful`

§§ `Treatment while I cannot decide (a situation: you lack capacity for a treatment decision)`

@export Is this treatment "significant treatment" (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 3)? It is if it involves a significant degree of bodily intrusion, a significant risk to me, significant side effects or significant distress; any other treatment is routine treatment.
GIVEN `intrusion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the treatment involves a significant degree of bodily intrusion
      `risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves a significant risk to you
      `side effects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves significant side effects
      `distress` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves significant distress to you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is this significant treatment` MEANS
    `s 3 -- significant treatment` `intrusion` `risk` `side effects` `distress`

@export May I be given this medical treatment while I do not have capacity to decide about it (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 48, 51, 54, 57-63)? My instructional directive binds (unless the practitioner may depart from it under s 51); otherwise my medical treatment decision maker decides; with neither, routine treatment may be given without consent and significant treatment only with the Public Advocate's consent. If I am likely to recover soon, treatment waits unless delay would significantly worsen my condition. Palliative care, special medical procedures and mental health treatment follow their own rules.
GIVEN `capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have decision-making capacity for this treatment decision
      `mental health patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a patient under the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022
      `kind` IS A `Kind of treatment` @desc What the treatment is: ordinary medical treatment; palliative care; a special medical procedure (such as sterilisation, under the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019); treatment for mental illness while you are a mental health patient; or neurosurgery for mental illness
      `intrusion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the treatment involves a significant degree of bodily intrusion
      `risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves a significant risk to you
      `side effects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves significant side effects
      `distress` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves significant distress to you
      `likely to recover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are likely to recover capacity for this decision within a reasonable time
      `delay worsens` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner reasonably believes further delay would significantly worsen your condition
      `directive located` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has located an advance care directive of yours
      `instruction` IS A `My instructional directive on this treatment` @desc What your instructional directive says about this treatment: nothing relevant; consents to it; or refuses it
      `changed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds that circumstances have changed so that your instructional directive no longer fits your preferences and values
      `VCAT delay worsens` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds that the delay of applying to VCAT would significantly worsen your condition
      `clinically appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner thinks the treatment clinically appropriate
      `decision maker located` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical treatment decision maker for you has been located (see the question on who it is)
      `decision` IS A `My decision maker's decision` @desc What your decision maker has decided: not yet; consents; or refuses
      `Public Advocate consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Advocate has consented to the treatment
GIVETH A `Whether I may be given the treatment`
`May I be given this treatment while I lack capacity` MEANS
    `Part 4 -- whether I may be given the treatment` `capacity` `mental health patient` `kind` `intrusion` `risk` `side effects` `distress` `likely to recover` `delay worsens` `directive located` `instruction` `changed circumstances` `VCAT delay worsens` `clinically appropriate` `decision maker located` `decision` `Public Advocate consents`

@export May I be given medical treatment without consent in an emergency (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 53)? Yes if the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds it is urgently necessary to save my life, prevent serious damage to my health, or prevent significant pain or distress, unless the practitioner knows I have refused that treatment. Electroconvulsive treatment is outside this section.
GIVEN `ECT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the treatment is electroconvulsive treatment
      `save life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds it is urgently necessary to save your life
      `prevent damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds it is urgently necessary to prevent serious damage to your health
      `prevent pain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believes on reasonable grounds it is urgently necessary to prevent significant pain or distress
      `refusal known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner is aware that you have refused this particular treatment (by an instructional directive or another valid, informed refusal)
GIVETH A `Whether I may be treated in an emergency without consent`
`May I be treated without consent in an emergency` MEANS
    `s 53 -- whether I may be treated in an emergency without consent` `ECT` `save life` `prevent damage` `prevent pain` `refusal known`

@export Must my health practitioner notify the Public Advocate that my decision maker refused significant treatment (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 62)? Yes, if the practitioner reasonably believes my preferences and values are not known to my decision maker and cannot be known or inferred.
GIVEN `refused significant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your medical treatment decision maker has refused significant treatment for you
      `preferences unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner reasonably believes your preferences and values are not known to, and cannot be known or inferred by, your decision maker
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my practitioner notify the Public Advocate` MEANS
    `s 62 -- the practitioner must notify the Public Advocate` `refused significant` `preferences unknown`

@export By when must the Public Advocate apply to VCAT to review my decision maker's refusal of significant treatment (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 67(2))? If it thinks the refusal unreasonable, as soon as practicable and not more than 14 days after it was notified.
GIVEN `notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Public Advocate received the practitioner's notification under section 62
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the Public Advocate apply to VCAT` MEANS
    `s 67(2) -- the last day for the Public Advocate to apply` `notified on`

@export Where does the Public Advocate's review of my decision maker's refusal stand (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 67)? If it thinks the refusal unreasonable it must apply to VCAT within 14 days after notification; if not, it must tell the practitioner no application will be made.
GIVEN `unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Advocate is of the opinion that the refusal is unreasonable in the circumstances
      `notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Public Advocate received the practitioner's notification under section 62
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Advocate has applied to VCAT
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day it applied (any date if it has not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where the Public Advocate's review stands`
`Where does the Public Advocate's review stand` MEANS
    `s 67 -- where the Public Advocate's review stands` `unreasonable` `notified on` `applied` `applied on` `today`

@export Is the health practitioner who treated me, or withheld treatment, protected from a claim by me (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 49, 52)? A practitioner who acted in good faith and without negligence is not liable in a civil proceeding if they believed on reasonable grounds that Part 4 was complied with, or acted on my instructional directive (even one revoked or invalid without their knowing). The practitioner's duty of care is unaffected.
GIVEN `good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner acted in good faith
      `no negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner acted without negligence
      `believed complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believed on reasonable grounds that the requirements of Part 4 of the Act were complied with
      `reliance` IS A `The directive the practitioner relied on` @desc Whether the practitioner relied on your instructional directive: did not; relied on it while it was in force; relied on it after it was revoked or while invalid, without knowing; or relied on it knowing it was revoked or invalid
      `believed in accordance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner believed on reasonable grounds that what was done accorded with that directive
GIVETH A `Whether the practitioner is protected`
`Is the practitioner protected from my claim` MEANS
    `s 52 -- whether the practitioner is protected` `good faith` `no negligence` `believed complied` `reliance` `believed in accordance`

§§ `Medical research while I cannot decide (a situation: you are asked to take part in research)`

@export May a medical research procedure be given to me while I do not have capacity to decide about it (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 72, 75, 77, 80, 85)? Only in a project approved by a human research ethics committee, only if I am not likely to recover capacity in time, and only with my consent in an instructional directive, or my decision maker's consent, or (if I have no decision maker) under the Division 3 conditions. Emergencies are under s 53.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have decision-making capacity for the procedure
      `approved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the research project has been approved by its human research ethics committee
      `likely to recover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are likely to recover capacity by the time the procedure would need to be given for the project
      `instruction` IS A `My instructional directive on this treatment` @desc What your instructional directive says about this procedure: nothing relevant; consents to it; or refuses it
      `has decision maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a medical treatment decision maker
      `decision` IS A `My decision maker's decision` @desc What your decision maker has decided about the procedure: not yet; consents; or refuses
      `Division 3 met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you have no decision maker: whether the researcher tried to find your directive and decision maker and believes on reasonable grounds every condition of section 80(1) is met
GIVETH A `Whether a research procedure may be given to me`
`May a research procedure be given to me` MEANS
    `Part 5 -- whether a research procedure may be given to me` `age` `capacity` `approved` `likely to recover` `instruction` `has decision maker` `decision` `Division 3 met`

§§ `A VCAT order has been made (a situation: VCAT made an order under this Act)`

@export Can I apply to VCAT for a rehearing of its order under this Act (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 88(1), (2))? A party at first instance may; a person given notice may with VCAT's leave. Interim or temporary orders, applications to suspend or revoke an appointment, medical research applications, orders by the President, and rehearing applications cannot be reheard.
GIVEN `standing` IS A `My standing for a rehearing` @desc Your part in the first hearing: a party; given notice but not a party; or neither
      `order` IS A `The order I want reheard` @desc The order you want reheard: a final order made at first instance; an interim or temporary order; an order on an application to suspend or revoke an appointment; an order on a medical research application (Part 5 Division 4); an order by VCAT constituted by the President; or an order on a rehearing or leave application
GIVETH A `Whether I may apply for a rehearing`
`Can I apply for a rehearing` MEANS
    `s 88(1), (2) -- whether I may apply for a rehearing` `standing` `order`

@export By when must I apply for a rehearing, or for leave to apply, of a VCAT order under this Act (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 88(3), (4))? Within 28 days after the day of the order; if VCAT gave oral reasons and a party then asked for written reasons, the 28 days run from the day the written reasons were given.
GIVEN `order made on` IS A DATE @desc The day VCAT made the order
      `written reasons requested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT gave oral reasons and a party then requested written reasons
      `reasons given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the written reasons were given to the party (any date if none were requested)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for a rehearing` MEANS
    `s 88(3) -- the last day to apply for a rehearing` (`s 88(3), (4) -- the day of the order` `order made on` `written reasons requested` `reasons given on`)

@export Is my application for a rehearing of a VCAT order under this Act in time (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) s 88(3), (4))? It must be made within 28 days after the day of the order (or of the written reasons, if requested after oral reasons). This Act gives VCAT no power to extend the time.
GIVEN `order made on` IS A DATE @desc The day VCAT made the order
      `written reasons requested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether VCAT gave oral reasons and a party then requested written reasons
      `reasons given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the written reasons were given to the party (any date if none were requested)
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied for a rehearing or for leave
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where my rehearing application stands`
`Is my rehearing application in time` MEANS
    `s 88(3) -- where my rehearing application stands` (`s 88(3), (4) -- the day of the order` `order made on` `written reasons requested` `reasons given on`) `applied` `applied on` `today`

§§ `Offences that protect me`

@export What is the maximum penalty for an offence against me under this Act (Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016 (Vic) ss 14, 15, 41, 42, 84, 85)? Inducing a directive or an appointment by dishonesty or undue influence, false statements about a directive, or pretending to be my decision maker or support person: 600 penalty units or 5 years' imprisonment or both (a body corporate 2400 penalty units, except under s 41). Research offences: 240 penalty units (and up to 2 years under s 85). The answer is in penalty units; 0 years means no imprisonment.
GIVEN `offence` IS A `Offence against me` @desc The offence: inducing your directive by dishonesty or undue influence; a false or misleading statement about your directive; pretending to be your decision maker or support person; inducing your appointment of a decision maker; research in an unapproved project; or research without consent or authorisation
      `offender` IS A `Kind of offender` @desc Whether the offender is a natural person or a body corporate
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
`What is the maximum penalty for an offence against me` MEANS
    `the maximum penalty` `offence` `offender`
